---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import SourcesNote from "../components/SourcesNote.astro";
import { figures } from "../data/site";

const compare = [
  { need: "Students", homes: `${figures.subdivisionStudents} new students`, campus: "Minimal new enrollment" },
  { need: "Daily traffic", homes: "A subdivision rush hour", campus: "Minimal once construction ends" },
  { need: "Emergency calls", homes: "A new neighborhood of calls", campus: "On-site security and fire suppression" },
  { need: "Water", homes: figures.subdivisionDailyWater, campus: "Closed loop. Pacifico: 23 LUEs" },
  { need: "Power", homes: "Every new kitchen and air conditioner", campus: "Pacifico generates 710 MW on site" },
  { need: "Tax relationship", homes: "Pays tax, then spends it", campus: "Pays tax, barely draws services" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Footprint"
  description="What Bastrop County data centers ask of residents: few students, little traffic, closed-loop water, and on-site power at the Pacifico campus."
>
  <PageHero
    kicker="The footprint"
    title="Four things a subdivision consumes. A data center is built to avoid them."
    lede="Residents are asked for very little ongoing service: not a school expansion, not a new rush hour, not a municipal water customer at neighborhood scale, and not — at Pacifico — a new load on the shared grid."
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-14 md:px-6 md:py-20">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-3xl border border-line bg-paper">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[40rem] text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Service demand of a 1,000-home subdivision compared with a data center</caption>
        <thead class="bg-pine text-paper">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium" scope="col">What residents provide</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium" scope="col">1,000 homes</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium" scope="col">Data center</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            compare.map((row) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-line">
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium" scope="row">{row.need}</th>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">{row.homes}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.campus}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
      The subdivision column is the comparison published by Building Bastrop County and Tomorrow Begins Here, not a
      specific plat already approved on these tracts.
    </p>

    <div class="copy mt-16 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-4xl">Water</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Modern halls planned for this county cool with a closed loop. Liquid picks up heat at the servers, a heat
            exchanger moves that heat out, and the same water goes around again. Legacy evaporative cooling is the
            design people picture when they hear “data center.” It is not the design these sources describe for
            Bastrop.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pacifico’s published potable use is about {figures.pacificoDailyWater} a day, counted as {figures.pacificoLues} living-unit equivalents. A residential equivalent of that campus is put at {figures.residentialLues} LUEs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tomorrow Begins Here sets a modern closed-loop campus near {figures.campusWater}. An 18-hole golf course
            is about {figures.golfWater}. A 1,000-home subdivision is {figures.subdivisionDailyWater}. At the top of
            that range, 400,000 gallons a day is about 146 million gallons a year — roughly 29 times the closed-loop
            campus figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nationally, every U.S. data center together used {figures.usFreshwater} of freshwater in 2023, about 15
            times less than what leaks from municipal pipes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-4xl">Power</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Pacifico is the straightforward case. The campus includes {figures.pacificoPower} of natural gas
            generation on the property. Building Bastrop County describes that as zero draw on the ERCOT grid and no
            rate impact from the design.
          </p>
          <p>
            EdgeConneX is a different electrical project: a hyperscale campus of up to {figures.edgeCapacity}, under
            construction, not described as an off-grid plant. For grid-connected industry, Tomorrow Begins Here points
            to Texas SB 6. Large loads can be ordered down sharply in a grid emergency — the published example is
            from 100 megawatts to 5 — so homes and hospitals stay served. The same account says large customers pay
            for the substations and lines their load requires, rather than handing that construction to residential
            ratepayers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-4xl">Schools and roads</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            A 1,000-home subdivision adds {figures.subdivisionStudents} students and a daily commute. The operating
            staff of a data center does not. Construction years are busy. Operating years are a specialized crew, not
            a thousand driveways emptying onto the same road at 7 a.m.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is why the tax payment is so efficient. The district receives commercial value and does not receive
            the enrollment that usually comes attached to it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-display text-4xl">Emergency services</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            The buildings watch themselves: on-site security and fire suppression are part of how a data center is
            operated. The county is not staffing a new residential beat inside the fence.
          </p>
          <p>
            The tax payment still reaches the emergency districts and the county. It buys equipment and people for
            the households that were already here, because the campus is not spending that payment on its own new
            population.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <SourcesNote />
  </article>
</Layout>
